{
  "id": "Compound::DB00543 - Gene::6530",
  "verbalization": "Amoxapine, a tricyclic antidepressant, is linked to Slc6a2 through a shared pathway involving Protriptyline, a related compound. The evidence suggests that Amoxapine causes various adverse effects, including Adenitis, Taste Peculiar, and Black Hairy Tongue, all of which are mediated by Protriptyline. Protriptyline binds to Slc6a2, indicating a potential mechanism by which these effects are initiated. The lowest common ancestor of Amoxapine and Protriptyline is the Tricyclic Antidepressant class and the Organic Tricyclic Compound category, reinforcing the structural and functional relationship between these entities. The consistent involvement of Protriptyline in the pathway, along with the binding of Protriptyline to Slc6a2, suggests that Amoxapine may influence Slc6a2 through Protriptyline, potentially affecting ion transport or other cellular processes. This mechanism highlights the role of Protriptyline as a bridge between Amoxapine and Slc6a2, underscoring the importance of understanding the pharmacological interactions within this pathway.",
